229. Thus it is said:

“Neither scattered nor deranged (Sī.
Pī.
), nor waiting for analysis;

Upright, so, with straightforward mind and loving eye, he looks upon the multitude.

“In happy states, he experiences the maturation of karma, there he rejoices;

Here he becomes a cowherd, with dark blue eyes, a handsome man.

“Advocates and skilled, many knowing omens;

Men skilled in subtle guidance,

they point out "beloved to see".

"Beloved to see, even as a householder, peaceful and,

he becomes beloved and dear to many people;

if he does not become a householder and becomes a renunciate,

he is dear to many, destroying sorrow."

(23) The characteristic of the uṇhīṣa and the head.

273. Thus it was said by the Blessed One.

Having said this, the Well-Gone One, the Teacher, said further:

“Parents are the east, teachers are the south;

Wife and children are the west, friends and colleagues are the north.

Servants and workers are below, ascetics and Brahmins are above;

These directions all should be honoured by a clansman true.

The wise, accomplished in virtue, gentle and eloquent;

Humble, firm in purpose, such a one obtains glory.

Energetic, not lazy, not wavering in adversity;

Of unbroken conduct, intelligent, such a one obtains glory.

Affectionate, making friends, articulate, without stinginess;

A leader, a trainer, a guide, such a one obtains glory.

Generosity, kind speech, beneficial action, and here,

Impartiality in all things, according to circumstances;

These kind dispositions hold the world together, Like the chariot’s axle-pin.

If such things did not exist, No mother from her son

Would receive honor or respect, Nor a father from his child.

Because these kind dispositions are esteemed by the wise,

Therefore they attain greatness, and they are ones who are praised.”

277. Homage to Vipassi (Vipassī) the blessed one, the one with the eyes, the glorious one.

Homage to Sikhi (Sikhī) also, the compassionate one to all beings.

Homage to Vessabhu (Vessabhū) also, the cleansed ascetic;

Homage to Kakusandha (Kakusandha), who has crushed Māra’s (māra) army.

Homage to Konagamana (Koṇāgamana), the brahmin, the one who has lived the holy life;

Homage to Kassapa (Kassapa) also, the one who is released in every way.

Homage to Angirasa (Aṅgīrasa), the glorious son of the Sakyas (Śākya);

Who taught this Dharma (dharma), the remover of all suffering (sarva-duḥkha).

Those in the world, who have extinguished , and have perceived through insight , things as they really are,

they never slander anyone; they are mighty men who are free from fear.

The good of gods and humans, is that to which Gotama is revered;

Endowed with knowledge and conduct, the great one, free from fear.

278. From where the sun comes up (sūriyo), the son of Aditi, the great circle,

When he is rising, twilight disappears;

When the sun has set, it is called "day."

There is also a deep lake there, an ocean where the waters have flowed;

“Thus in that place they know there is an ocean where the waters have flowed."

"From here that is the Easterly direction, so the people declare;

That direction is watched over by a resplendent Great King.

The overlord of the gandhabbas, by the name of Dhṛtarāṣṭra;

He delights in dance and song, attended by gandhabbas.

He has many sons, so I have heard, all with the same name;

They are eighty and ten and one, Inda by name, ones of great strength,

Having seen the Buddha, the kinsman of the sun;

From afar, do reverence him, who is great and fully mature:

"Homage to you, noble man, homage to you, best of men;

You consider with skill, even non-humans worship you;

This is often heard, therefore we speak thus.

'Worship the Victor Gotama, we worship the Victor Gotama;

Endowed with knowledge and conduct, we worship the Buddha Gotama.'"

279. There they say (go) the departed, who speak maliciously, backbiters.

The murderers, the cruel, thieves, the deceitful people.

From here that is the Southerly direction, so the people declare;

That direction is watched over by a resplendent Great King.

Chief of the *kumbhāṇḍas*, his name is "Virūḷha";

He delights in dance and song, attended by *kumbhāṇḍas*.

He has many sons, I have heard, with one name;

They are eighty and ten and one, Inda by name, ones of great strength.

They too, seeing the Buddha, the Buddha, kinsman of the sun,

From afar they pay homage, to the great one, devoid of arrogance.

Homage to you, o thoroughbred man! Homage to you, o highest among men!

You consider with skill, even non-humans venerate you;

This is not heard frequently, therefore thus we shall speak.

'We reverence Gotama, the Conqueror, we reverence Gotama;

Perfected in knowledge and conduct, we reverence the Buddha Gotama.'

280. Where the sun goes down, the son of Aditi, the great circle,

When the sun is going down, the day ceases;

And after the sun has come up, it is said to be ‘*saṃvarī*.’

“Even a deep lake is there, the ocean with its river water;

Thus, they know it there, ‘the ocean with its river water.’

“ ‘This is the western direction,’ thus people tell it;

That direction is watched over by a resplendent Great King.

The overlord of the Nāgas, his name is ‘Virūpakkha’;

He delights in dance and song, honored by the Nāgas.

“He also has many sons, with one name, so I have heard;

They are eighty and ten and one, Inda by name, ones of great strength.

When they have seen the Buddha, the Buddha, kinsman of the sun,

From afar, they pay homage, the great one, free from arrogance.

“Homage to you, thoroughbred man, homage to you, best of men;

You regard with skill, even non-humans venerate you;

This is not heard often, therefore thus we speak.

“ ‘Venerate the Conqueror Gotama, we venerate the Conqueror Gotama;

Accomplished in knowledge and conduct, we venerate the Buddha Gotama.’

283. "Which are the *yakṣas*, the great *yakṣas*, the generals (*senāpati*), the great generals (*mahāsenāpati*)?

"Indra, Soma, Varuṇa, Bharadvāja, Prajāpati;

Candana, Kāmaśreṣṭha, Kinnughaṇḍu, Nighaṇḍu.

"Panāda, Opamañña, Devasūta, Mātali;

Cittasena, the *gandharva*, Nala the king, Janesabha.

"Sātāgira of Himavat, Puṇṇaka, Karatiya, Guḷa;

Sīvaka, Mucalinda, Vessāmitta, Yugandhara.

"Gopāla, Supparodha, Hiri, Netti, Mandiya;

Pañcālacaṇḍa, Āḷavaka, Pajjunna, Sumana, Sumukha;

Dadhīmukha, Maṇi, Māṇivara, Dīgha, Atho Serīsaka Saha.

"Then to these *yakṣas*, great *yakṣas*, generals, and great generals, one should call out, one should shout out, one should cry out: ‘This *yakṣa* has seized me, this *yakṣa* has grabbed me, this *yakṣa* annoys me, this *yakṣa* harasses me, this *yakṣa* hurts me, this *yakṣa* injures me, this *yakṣa* will not release me.’

“This, dear Sir (*mārisa*), is the Āṭānāṭiyā protection, for the monks’ (*bhikkhu*), nuns’ (*bhikkhunī*), laymen’s (*upāsaka*), and laywomen’s (*upāsikā*) guard, protection, freedom from harm (*avihiṃsā*), and comfortable living (*phāsuvihāra*).

“Come then, dear Sir (*mārisa*), let us go, we have many duties, and there is much which ought to be done.”

“Now is the time for whatever you Great Kings (*mahārājāno*) are thinking.”

287. Homage to Vipassī (vipassissa ca namatthu), possessed of the eye (cakkhumantassa) and splendor (sirīmato).

Homage to Sikhi (sikhissapi ca namatthu), who is compassionate (sabbabhūtānukampino) to all beings (sabba-bhūta).

Homage to Vessabhu (vessabhussa ca namatthu), who has bathed (nhātakassa), the ascetic (tapassino);

Homage to Kakusandha (namatthu kakusandhassa), the destroyer (apamaddino) of Māra's army (mārasenā).

Homage to Koṇāgamana (koṇāgamanassa namatthu), the Brāhmaṇa (brāhmaṇassa) of accomplished desires (vusīmato);

Homage to Kassapa (kassapassa ca namatthu), who is fully liberated (vippamuttassa) in every way (sabbadhi).

Homage to Aṅgīrasa (aṅgīrasassa namatthu), the son of the Śākya (sakyaputtassa), the glorious one (sirīmato);

He who taught (desesi) this Dharma (dhammaṃ), the dispeller (apanūdanaṃ) of all suffering (sabbadukkhā).

Those who are extinguished (nibbutā) in the world (loke), who have perceived (vipassisuṃ) things as they really are (yathābhūtaṃ);

Those people (te janā) are without malice (apisuṇātha), great (mahantā), and free from anxiety (vītasāradā).

The good (hitaṃ) of gods (deva) and humans (manussānaṃ), they revere (namassanti) Gotama (gotamaṃ);

Accomplished (sampannaṃ) in knowledge (vijjā) and conduct (ācaraṇa), great (mahantaṃ), and free from anxiety (vītasāradaṃ).

289. ‘There they say (go) the departed, who speak maliciously, backbiters.

Killers of creatures (*pāṇātipātino*), hunters (*luddā*), thieves (*corā*), and fraudulent people (*nekatikā janā*).

‘From here that is the Southerly direction (*dakkhiṇā disā*), so the people declare;

That direction is watched over by a resplendent Great King (*mahārājā yasassi*),

‘The overlord of the Kumbhāṇḍas, his name is Virūḷha (*virūḷho*);

He delights in dance and song, surrounded by Kumbhāṇḍas.

‘Even he has many sons; so I have heard, they are of one name;

Eighty and ten and one, Inda (*inda*) by name, ones of great strength (*mahabbalā*).

‘And they too, having seen the Buddha, the Buddha, kinsman of the sun;

From afar they pay homage, to the great one, devoid of arrogance.

‘Homage to thee, who art unique among mankind (*purisājañña*); glory to thee, the noblest among men (*purisuttama*)!’

You perceive with skill, even non-humans bow down to you;

This is heard not infrequently; therefore, thus we speak.

‘‘Pay homage to the Conqueror, Gotama (*gotama*), we pay homage to the Conqueror, Gotama;

Endowed with knowledge and conduct, we pay homage to the Buddha, Gotama’’.

That place where the sun goes down, the son of Aditi, the great circle.

When the sun is setting, the day ceases;

When the sun has set, it is called "Saṃvarī."

Even a deep lake is there, a sea with flowing water;

Thus they know it there, a sea with flowing water.

'From here is "that western direction"', thus people declare it;

Whatever direction the glorious great king protects.

And of the Nāgas the <pb ed="v" n="3. 0160"/> lord, "Virūpakkha" by name;

<pb ed="v" n="3. 0160"/> He delights in dance and song, honored by the Nāgas.

'He had many sons, I have heard, each with one name;

They are eighty and ten and one, Inda by name, ones of great strength.

'They too, having seen the Buddha, the Buddha, kinsman of the sun;

From afar they worship, the great one, devoid of essence.

'Homage to you, thoroughbred man, homage to you, best of men;

You consider skillfully, even non-humans worship you;

This is heard not infrequently, therefore thus we speak.

"Worship the Conqueror Gotama, we worship the Conqueror Gotama;

Accomplished in knowledge and conduct, we worship the Buddha Gotama"'.

291. Where lies delightful Uttarakuru, where towers beautiful Mount Meru.

People are born there, without ego, without possessions.

They do not sow the seed, and nor are there ploughs led along,

Humans enjoy rice that ripens without plowing.

Husked, without chaff, pure, fragrant with the fruit of rice,

Having cooked it in a pot, then they eat food.

Having made a cow with a single hoof, they go in every direction;

Having made an animal with a single hoof, they go in every direction.

Having made a woman a vehicle, they go in every direction;

Having made a man a vehicle, they go in every direction.

Having made a girl a vehicle, they go in every direction;

Having made a boy a vehicle, they go in every direction.

They, having mounted a vehicle,

Go around in all directions;

The attendants of that king.

Elephant vehicles, horse vehicles,

Divine vehicles are present;

Palaces and litters,

Of the glorious great king.

And his cities were,

Well-measured in the sky;

Āṭānāṭā, Kusināṭā, Parakusināṭā,

Nāṭasuriyā, Parakusiṭanāṭā.

To the north are farmers,

And a multitude of people to the west;

Navanavatiya, Ambara-ambaravatiya,

Ālakamandā by name, the king's capital.

For Kuvera, good sir, the great king, Visāṇā by name is the capital;

Therefore Kuvera, the great king, is called "Vessavaṇa."

Sending forth, they proclaim, Tatolā, Tattalā, Tatotalā;

Ojasi, Tejasi, Tatojasi, Sūra, Rājā, Ariṭṭha, Nemi.

There also a lake, Dharaṇī by name, from which the clouds rain;

From which the rains descend, and there is also a hall, Sālavatī by name.

Where the *yakṣas* attend, there are trees with constant fruit;

Various flocks of birds are joined, resounding with peacocks and herons;

With the lovely calls of cuckoos and others.

There, the sound of the *jīvañjīvaka* bird, and also *oṭṭhava* and *cittaka*;

*Kukkuṭaka* and *kuḷīraka*, in the forest, *pokkharasātaka*.

There, the sound of *śuka* and *sāḷika*, and *daṇḍamāṇavaka*;

That Kuvera's lotus pool always shines at all times.

"From here is the northern direction," thus people declare;

The direction that the glorious great king protects.

And the lord of the *yakṣas*, "Kuvera" by name;

He delights in dance and song, revered by the *yakṣas*.

And he has many sons, with a single name, so I have heard;

Eighty, ten, and one, Inda by name, of great strength.

And they, having seen the Buddha, the Buddha, kinsman of the sun;

From afar they pay homage, the great one, devoid of arrogance.

Homage to you, thoroughbred man, homage to you, best of men;

You perceive with skill, even non-humans worship you;

This is heard not just once, therefore we say thus.

"Worship the Victor, Gotama, we worship the Victor, Gotama;

Endowed with knowledge and conduct, we worship the Buddha, Gotama."

293. To which *yakkha*s, great *yakkha*s (*mahāyakkha*s), generals (*senāpati*) and great generals (*mahāsenāpati*)?

‘Indra, Soma, Varuṇa, Bharadvaja, Prajāpati;

Candana, Kāmaśreṣṭha, Kinnughaṇḍu, Nighaṇḍu;

Panāda, Opamañña, Devasūta, and Mātali,

Cittasena the *gandharva*, Naḷa the king, and Janeśabha;

Sātāgira, Haimavata, Puṇṇaka, Karatiya, Guḷa;

Śivaka, Mucalinda, Veśyamitra, Yugandhara;

Gopāla, Supparodha, Hiri, Netti, Mandiya;

Pañcālacaṇḍa, Āḷavaka, Pajjunna, Sumana, Sumukha;

Dadhimukha, Maṇi, Māṇivara, Dīgha, and Serīsaka together.

“To these *yakkha*s, great *yakkha*s, generals, and great generals, one should call out, one should shout out, one should cry out: ‘This *yakkha* has seized me, this *yakkha* has grabbed me, this *yakkha* annoys me, this *yakkha* harasses me, this *yakkha* hurts me, this *yakkha* injures me, this *yakkha* will not release me.’

This, dear Sir (*mārisa*), is the Āṭānāṭiya protection, for the monks’ (*bhikkhu*s), nuns’ (*bhikkhunī*s), laymen’s (*upāsaka*s), and laywomen’s (*upāsikā*s) guard, protection, freedom from harm (*avihiṃsā*), and comfortable living (*phāsuvihāra*).

And now, dear Sir (*mārisa*), we shall go, as we have many duties, and there is much which ought to be done.”

“Now is the time for whatever you Great Kings (*mahārājāna*) are thinking.”
